Byron A Jeff wrote:
>
> >Getting your PW up is a key goal for spellcasters.
>
> I do this as I find better spells. When I play neutrals I find that
> the Eye of the Aethiopica is great for keeping power up. I have one in
> my current game as a monk. Virtually unlimited spellcasting abilities.

PW increase is yet another benefit of leveling up.  Not much
relevance to that aspect for a combat wombat but it matters
greatly for a spell caster.  It can even matter more than the
increased number of skill slots.

> >Getting poison resistance is a key goal.
>
> Critical.

I'll give it priority at least as high as AC points.

> >Getting sleep resistance is a key goal.
>
> Not as critical but important.

But it comes from elves who are a sort of nice equipment for
early characters.  Kill for the cloak or dagger, get sleep
resistance as a bonus.

> >Getting reflection and magic resistance are key goals.
>
> Critical. I'd put them 3rd on your list.

But they aren't all that likely until the mid-game.  High priority
doesn't mean they can be expected before finishing Sokoban.

> >Getting MC3 is a key goal
>
> Question. If you have MR do you still need MC3?

There is very little overlap between the two.

Werefoo bite?  MR irrelevant MC matters.  Polymorph trap?
MC irrelevant MR matters.  There's a long list of things one
but not the other will handle.

> >Getting magic
> >resistance before the castle/Quest is only guaranteed for wizards,
>
> Also reflection is more helpful anyway. You can get far with reflection
> and MC3.

The overlap between what reflection amd MR protect against is
larger than the overlap between MC and MR.

> >That with, say, 4 points of protection gets you to AC0 and some MC.  It
> >doesn't take much of a lucky find (say, dwarvish mithril, or finding
> >one of your pieces of armor at +2 plus some studded leather) to have
> >your AC down to a fairly comfortable AC -5 or lower.

Studded leather for spellcasters - The watch tends to carry them
and your strong mean pet takes them out.

Mithril for combat wombats - Either dwarves or elves.

> But with protection you don't have a to carry all of it either.

Armor plus protection gives better AC than armor or protection, at
the cost of more weight.

> >Wishing up DSM or other "top 10%" luck can have you even better off.
>
> Easily AC -20. At which point you can walk around knowing that a
> first hit isn't an instadeath.

Until you start seeing minotaurs.  ;^)

> >But lack of PR can potentially interfere with getting there (or staying
> >there).  Getting a unicorn horn helps a lot since you can eat poisonous
> >corpses with impunity (and the unicorn's corpse could grant PR itself).
>
> A unihorn is high priority in the part of the game.

Two IMO.  I like to dip potions to identify the bad ones and that
first potion of polymorph can be quite a surprise.  But the hurdle
with unihorns is managing to get the first one.  After you can
more are comparatively easy.

> >  A tinning kit may be handy.  Any food shops
> >with ice chests that you've found (or random ice chests in the dungeon)
>
> Rare. Very very rare.

But not by the late game.  I'll leave a few floating eyes and such
behind after getting enough intrinsics.  Then if I get a tinning kit
I cycle back up and get a bunch of canned monsters for the
various intrinsics.

> >Also at around this point elves can start showing up.  They don't
> >respect E, so you should run for choke points (corridors, whatever) or
> >the stairs when they appear.
>
> For some reason elves never were a big deal to me.

They carry good early game equipment.  By the late game a
few tins of elf meat in case you lose your sleep resistance
and they aren't interesting.  But early on every elf has a
chance of adding one elven dagger to your quiver.

> Stressed only because I just found a bones pile with my first bag and I'm
> shifting stuff around in prep to move on.

I try to shuttle stuff Burdened not Stressed.  And drop a bunch
of heavy items for any combat.

> Or a pickaxe. Essential equipment. Pick one up in the mines.

I consider pickaxe important enough that I will sometimes spend
one skill slot advancing it for classes that can.  One more
variation in skill slot management strategy.

> Levelling up isn't free. Truthfully neither is the AC. But properly managed
> it can be low cost and serve you in multiple capacities for the rest of the
> game (lower AC/weight ration, lower hit percentage, lower damage/hit).

For a spell caster - Get create monster and altar camping will beat
any protection racket.  Doesn't apply to many classes but it works
better as it costs corpses not gold.



